<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Yusuf Yaqub ibn Abd al-Haqq</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1212–1286</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Yusuf Yaqub ibn Abd al-Haqq was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> from 1258 to 1286. In 1269 he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, ending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> rule, and in 1276 he founded the royal city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fes-el-jdid">Fes el-Jdid</a> beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>. He crossed several times to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> to support the Nasrids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-yusuf-yaqub-ibn-abd-al-haqq.jpg" alt="Abu Yusuf Yaqub ibn Abd al-Haqq" width="320" height="233" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfonso X (Life time: 1221-1284) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MoorandChristianBattle.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Abu Yusuf Yaqub was the fourth son of the Marinid founder Abd al-Haqq I and of Oum el-Iman bint Ali el-Bethary, a Zenata woman. When his brother Abu Yahya died in July 1258, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and eastern and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> kept the south around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>. Abu Yahya had named his own son as heir, but Abu Yusuf, then governor of Taza, displaced his nephew and was acknowledged as emir.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In September 1260 a Christian fleet, probably Castilian, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a>, which Abu Yusuf retook after a fourteen-day siege. His first assault on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> in 1262 failed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> chieftain Abu Dabbus, whom he then backed, turned against him and drew in Yaghmorassan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>. Abu Yusuf defeated the Abdalwadids by the Moulouya in 1268 and took the title Prince of the Muslims, never claiming the caliphal title. The Draa valley submitted in 1271, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ceuta">Ceuta</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangiers</a> in 1273 and Sijilmassa in 1274, a siege in which Ibn Khaldoun records what seems to be one of the first uses of powder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>.</p><p>In 1275, answering an appeal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ii-of-granada">Muhammad II al-Faqih</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, he crossed the straits, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> and defeated Castilian armies at the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecija">Écija</a> and the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martos">Martos</a>. He crossed again in 1277, 1282 and 1285, shifting alliances between Granada, the Banu Ashqilula and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-x-of-castile">Alfonso X of Castile</a>. His son Abu Yaqub won the Battle of Algeciras on 21 July 1279. In October 1285 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iv-of-castile">Sancho IV of Castile</a> agreed a five-year truce and handed over Arabic books seized from Andalusian libraries. Abu Yusuf fell ill and died at Algeciras in March 1286 while negotiating with Granada, and was buried in the Marinid necropolis at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chellah">Chellah</a>, which he had built.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Although Abd al-Haqq or Abu Yahya are often named as founders of the dynasty, Abu Yusuf is regarded as the founder of the Marinid state: he ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>, unified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, built Fes el-Jedid, first called the White City, opposite the old Idrisid city, and gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a> a foothold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He left the state fragile, however. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> and Maqil of the south were only half subdued, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">High Atlas</a> unconvinced, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> still a threat. Without the religious prestige of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire">Almoravids</a> or Almohads, the Marinids remained at root a tribal dynasty.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Yusuf Yaqub ibn Abd al-Haqq” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fez, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1260" aria-label="Fez on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Abu Yusuf Yaqub ibn Abd al-Haqq"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4646 4647h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4646 4647h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1260">What was here in 1260? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
